@thvasilo Answering your question from January first, sorry it sat this long: yes, the adapter was duplicated. `actor/lora_adapter/` came from the pre-existing dump in `fsdp_workers.py::save_checkpoint`, written from the live `peft_config` (correct `lora_alpha` — your 1.2K `adapter_config.json`). My `huggingface/lora_adapter/` was a second copy with a config reconstructed from tensor shapes (`lora_alpha=0` — the 1.1K one). Same weights, but my copy had the worse config; if you kept using `actor/lora_adapter/` you were fine. The duplicate dump is gone in the update.

Your 2026-03-31 read of #5326 is also correct. #5326 only persists `lora_train_meta.json` and teaches the offline `model_merger` to use it for `lora_alpha`; it never touched the checkpoint-time HF save. On current main, `FSDPCheckpointManager.save_checkpoint` still does `get_fsdp_full_state_dict` → `save_pretrained(hf_local_path, state_dict=state_dict)` (`verl/utils/checkpoint/fsdp_checkpoint_manager.py:344,381`) with no PEFT key handling, so `huggingface/` still contains `base_model.model.*` / `*.base_layer.weight` keys plus raw `lora_A/lora_B` tensors. Two things changed since this PR was written: `fsdp_workers.py` was deleted in the engine refactor, so main no longer writes *any* `lora_adapter/` at save time; and main gained `normalize_peft_param_name` (`verl/utils/fsdp_utils.py:842`) doing exactly this PR's key normalization — but it's only wired into rollout weight sync, not checkpointing.

I've rebuilt the branch on current main accordingly: (1) the HF save path now normalizes the PEFT state dict by reusing `normalize_peft_param_name`, and (2) a single `lora_adapter/` is written whose config comes from the real `peft_config` (no reconstruction, no copy under `huggingface/`). CPU tests cover the load round-trip and the config sourcing. Since every original code site died in the refactor, this is effectively a new diff under the old PR number.

@vermouth1992 the defect still reproduces on main (`huggingface/` output from LoRA runs is not loadable by vLLM/HF); PTAL.

